  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1215 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nLong-term forecast impacts: Drier forecast and a gradual warm-up \nthroughout the week.\n\nThe trough zips out of the forecast area on Monday and is replaced \nby amplified shortwave ridging. Cloud cover will likely linger \nduring much of the day on Monday and keep temperatures below normal. \nBy Tuesday, the next large-scale trough across the West will begin \ninfluencing the surface pattern on the Plains by inducing lee \ntroughing in the Dakotas/Wyoming/Montana. This will gradually cause \nwinds to shift to southerly across the forecast area. However, the \nwarmup that follows will likely be less-dramatic than we have seen \nagain and again in 2026 because surface winds may not contain much \nof a westerly component, and because there will actually be cool air \ncirculating even in the warm advection regime. Therefore, even by \nWednesday highs will only be reaching around 60 in our area.\n\nThe warm advection regime becomes a little more pronounced by \nThursday and into the end of the week, sending temperatures back \nabove normal. Low-end precipitation chances creep back into the \nforecast on Thursday as well, but ensemble data have been \npinpointing next weekend (in the Day 8 - Day 10 timeframe) as one of \ngreater probability for impactful precipitation.\n\nMeister",
